d report what changed. Do\012  not repeat unchanged discovery or failed checks merely to produce activity.\012  Preserve only the current review and its actionable reason in hot state; older\012  versions are already retained by runner-owned archived snapshots.\012\012Select the acceptance gate:\012\0121. Read the source roadmap, then the maintained Markdown checkbox checklist and\012   compact current handoff in the tracking file. Consult historical entries for\012   evidence, not as a competing task list. If no checklist exists, create it in\012   the tracking file from the original roadmap's acceptance gates without changing\012   their scope or deleting history. An empty tracking scaffold is not completion.\0122. Resume the current handoff active unchecked gate. If none is named, choose the first\012   dependency-ready unchecked gate in roadmap order. Before editing, state its\012   exact checkbox ID/text, acceptance criteria and necessary prerequisites.\012   Obey any stricter per-iteration rule written in the roadmap itself. For example,\012   if the roadmap says each run may process only one checkbox, do not complete,\012   check, or otherwise process a second checkbox in the same invocation.\0123. Compare the current handoff and latest supervisor review with the actual\012   repository state. Do not load prior iteration narratives merely to reconstruct\012   chronology. Resolve the missing active-gate dependency when it is authorized\012   and feasible; size or difficulty alone is not a blocker. If it needs unavailable access, an external service, user\012   judgment or another unmet dependency, leave its checkbox open and record the\012   evidence, unblock condition and next ready checkbox. Continue other useful\012   authorized work in this invocation if coding has not started, or hand it off\012   for the next iteration after finishing the current coherent batch.\0124. Revisit a deferred gate only when its unblock condition changes or a planned\012   bounded retry is due. Keep a compact deferred-gates list in the handoff so\012   fresh contexts do not repeat discovery or silently forget the original gate.\012   Resume it when ready; do not replace its criteria with smaller adjacent tests.\012\0125. Before coding, write a Batch plan in the current handoff: one concrete outcome,\012   original parent gate, related child IDs, shared harness/setup, combined checks\012   and explicit session exit criteria. Size this plan for roughly 30 minutes of\012   implementation. Newly discovered children remain steps toward this outcome;\012   completing one child cannot redefine the selected batch as finished.\0126. For integration work, compose the required canonical harness first, including\012   real middleware, transport and source families required by the selected proof.\012   Reuse it across related cases in the same session. If an essential boundary\012   remains prepared, carry that gap as unfinished batch work and address it before\012   final validation when feasible. A narrower prepared test is prerequisite\012   evidence, not a reason to defer the missing integration boundary indefinitely.\012\012Implement and verify:\012\012- Target roughly 30 minutes of implementation per invocation, excluding initial\012  discovery and final verification. This is a planning target, not a hard deadline\012  or a minimum to fill with unnecessary work. Plan several related ready child\012  tasks under one parent gate that share code, context and verification.\012- For larger roadmaps, the recommended total worker-session budget is **1 hour**:\012  discovery, implementation, feature verification and compact handoff all fit\012  inside it. Use that allowance to deliver a meaningful feature or coherent\012  milestone, not isolated fragments followed by another bootstrap. The approximate\012  30-minute implementation target is a planning aid, not a mandatory exit point.\012  Leave time for verification and handoff before the configured tim